course: dictionary
lesson: 16118
title: Old jobs in old words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A wheelwright is a person who makes and mends wooden wheels. You can often work out a word by looking at its parts. Wheel is the first part, and a wheelwright works on wheels. «The wheelwright spent all morning mending the wheel of the farm cart.» The sentence tells you that the job is about carts and wheels, so the meaning fits.
explain: The second part, wright, is an old word for a worker who makes or builds something. When you see a word with an old-fashioned part like this, ask yourself what job or thing the first part names. «The farmer took his broken cart to the wheelwright, who had it working again by evening.» Broken and working again are the clues: the wheelwright is the one who fixes carts.
explain: Context helps you to test your guess. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the hard word. «Every village needed a wheelwright.» If you read it as «Every village needed a person who makes and mends wheels», it still makes sense, so your meaning is right. A dictionary will confirm it.
fact: A wheelwright is a person who makes and repairs wooden wheels for carts, wagons and carriages.
fact: Before cars, every village needed a wheelwright, together with a blacksmith who fitted iron tyres onto the wheels.
fact: In «The Wind in the Willows», Toad's caravan and the carts of the countryside were made by craftsmen like wheelwrights.
fact: Surnames like Wright and Wainwright come from old jobs. A Wainwright was a maker of wagons.
fact: Many words in English have hidden clues: look at the parts of the word and the sentence around it.
exercise: «The wheelwright spent all morning mending the wheel of the farm cart.» What is a wheelwright? | *A person who makes and repairs wooden wheels | A farmer who grows wheat | A person who sells cars | A driver who races carts
exercise: What did the wheelwright mend in the sentence? | A bicycle chain | A glass window | *A wooden wheel | A car engine
exercise: Before cars, every village needed a wheelwright and which other craftsman? | A dentist | A pilot | A postman | *A blacksmith
test: Which surname comes from an old job of making wagons? | *Wainwright | Thompson | Brown | Carter's cousin
test: Why would a farmer take his cart to a wheelwright? | *To get the wheels mended | To get his hair cut | To buy a new car | To learn to read
exam: A wheelwright is an old job; what is the best clue to its meaning? | *The word wheel, because a wheelwright works with wheels | It rhymes with light, so it means bright | The word wright, which is a kind of animal | It must mean a rich person
